==English==
{{was wotd|2010|March|27}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|bilieux}}, from {{uder|en|la|bīliōsus||full of bile}}, from {{m|la|bīlis||bile}} + {{m|la|-ōsus||full of}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪl.i.əs/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-bilious.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɪl.jəs/|/ˈbɪl.i.əs/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪliəs|ɪljəs|s1=3|s2=2}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of or pertaining to something [[containing]] or [[consisting]] of [[bile]].
# Resembling bile, especially in color.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1820|author=w:Percy Bysshe Shelley|title=Oedipus Tyrannus; Or, Swellfoot The Tyrant: A Tragedy in Two Acts|text=Does money fail?—come to my mint—coin paper,<br>Till gold be at a discount, and ashamed<br>To show his '''bilious''' face, go purge himself,<br>In emulation of her vestal whiteness.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1845|author=w:Alexandre Dumas|title=w:Twenty Years After|chapter=31|passage=His complexion was pale, not of that deadly pallor which is a kind of neutral beauty, but of a '''bilious''', yellow hue; his colorless hair was short and scarcely extended beyond the circle formed by the hat around his head, and his light blue eyes seemed destitute of any expression.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=w:Sinclair Lewis|title=[[w:Main Street (novel)|Main Street]]|chapter=3|text=The business-center of Schoenstrom took up one side of one block, facing the railroad. It was a row of one-story shops covered with galvanized iron, or with clapboards painted red and '''bilious''' yellow.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1952|author=w:Ralph Ellison|title=w:Invisible Man|section=prologue|text=A beautiful girl once told me of a recurring nightmare in which she lay in the center of a large dark room and felt  her face expand until it filled the whole room, becoming a formless mass while her eyes ran in '''bilious''' jelly up the chimney.}}
# {{lb|en|pathology}} Suffering from real or supposed [[liver]] [[disorder]], especially excessive secretions of [[bile]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1815|author=w:Jane Austen|title=[[w:Emma (novel)|Emma]]|volume=II|section=chapter 7|passage=Perry tells me that Mr. Cole never touches malt liquor. You would not think it to look at him, but he is '''bilious'''—Mr. Cole is very '''bilious'''.}}
# Peevishly ill-humored, [[irritable]] or bad tempered; [[irascible]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 1830|author=w:Thomas Macaulay|title=[Review of] Southey's Colloquies on Society|work=The {{w|Edinburgh Review}}|page=536|text=The glorified spirit of a great statesman and philosopher dawdling, like a '''bilious''' old Nabob at a watering-place, over quarterly reviews and novels—dropping in to pay long calls—making excursions in search of the picturesque!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1934|author=w:George Orwell|title=w:Burmese Days|text=The boarders, sharp-tongued '''bilious''' widows, pursued the only man in the establishment, a mild, bald creature who worked in {{w|La Samaritaine}} {{...}}}}

